# Stop: a SOFTWARE stop, not an E-stop (ADR-0071 decision 4).
#
# Cancels the request in flight - the goal sent through the twin, the track
# held where each carriage stands (`RosCell.cancel`), every belt the console
# started commanded to zero - over the same command path everything else uses,
# and waits for each cancelled goal's end. A gripper motion already in progress
# on a physical side is NOT stopped by the cancel (ADR-0070 item 4): it runs to
# its end. It is no safety function: the physical E-stop is the only closure
# (cross-cutting-safety.md). After it the console does NOT home on its own
# (ADR-0037); it is READY once every end was read, or FAULT when the stop itself
# could not be confirmed. A stop that arrives after the request's last point
# that can be interrupted changes nothing: the request reports that it ended.
#
# In FAULT on a pair with a physical side, while the twin is not heard in SIM,
# Stop means "ask the twin for SIM again": it is accepted, and answered once
# the twin has answered. Refused with success false when nothing is in progress
# otherwise.
#
# Served under the plan's `console.stop` (ConsoleState.msg).
---
bool success   # a request was in progress and was asked to stop, or SIM was asked again
string detail  # or why there was nothing to stop
